================================================================================
AZRAVIBE ECOSYSTEM & AZRA (.vibe) PROGRAMMING LANGUAGE SPECIFICATION
================================================================================

Reference implementation: `Cargo.toml` v0.0.1 — a tree-walking interpreter in
Rust (see section 5). This document describes the language the interpreter
actually accepts; README.md is the short-form quick start.

1. ECOSYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & NAMING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Existing Farsi Language : Azravibe (.azr) -> Persian programming language & RTL IDE
- New English Language    : Azra (.vibe)    -> General-purpose & game scripting language
- Creator Studio          : Azravibe Studio -> 3D rounded-voxel builder, physics & IDE
- Game Client             : Azravibe Player -> Multiplayer game launcher & runtime

Visual & Gameplay Style:
- Rounded Voxels / Beveled Blocks (chamfered edges with procedural materials)
- Physics Assemblies (welded block groups, hinges, motors, springs, vehicles)
- Standalone + Embedded VM (runs via CLI `azra run app.vibe` or inside Studio)


2. CORE SYNTAX RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- No curly braces {}, square brackets [], or parentheses (). Lists are written
  as comma-separated values; blocks are written with indentation.
- Code blocks start with a colon `:` and use exactly 4 spaces per level. Tabs
  are rejected, and mixed/inconsistent indentation is a syntax error.
- Statements end with a newline. A single trailing period `.` is ignored
  (`say "hi".`).
- Function arguments and list items are separated by commas `,`.
- Identifiers: `[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*`. No spaces, no leading digits, no
  special symbols (`player_health`, `maxSpeed`). Keywords may be shadowed by
  variable names, but doing so is discouraged.
- Comments (three styles):
    note: comment text        line comment (`note:` also works after code)
    # comment text            line comment
    /* comment text */        block comment, may span several lines
  A comment-only line never changes the indentation of the surrounding block.
- String literals use `"` or `'` quotes. Escapes: `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\\`, `\"`,
  `\'`, `\{`, `\}`. `{dotted.path}` inside a string is interpolated at runtime
  (`say "HP: {player.health}"`); the path must consist of identifiers and the
  referenced variable must exist.
- Numbers: integers (`100`, i64) and floats (`12.5`, f64). `-5` is unary minus.
  Booleans are `true` / `false`; `null` / `nothing` are the empty value.


3. KEYWORDS & OPERATORS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3.1 Variables, objects and mutation
- set <target> to <value>          create or update a variable/property/key/item
- make <TYPE> as <NAME>            spawn a mock engine object (`rounded_box`,
                                    `sound`, `player`, ... in standalone mode)
- add <value> to <target>          number arithmetic or list append
- subtract <value> from <target>
- multiply <target> by <value>
- divide <target> by <value>
- remove <value> from <list>       remove the first matching item (no-op if absent)
- remove item <index> from <list>  remove by 1-based index
- remove key <key> from <map>
- sort <list> / reverse <list> / clear <list-or-map>

3.2 Console output
- say <values...>       print to stdout, values joined by spaces
- warn <values...>      print `[WARN] ...` to stderr
- error <values...>     print `[ERROR] ...` to stderr

3.3 Operators
- Arithmetic: +  -  *  /  modulo  power
- Comparison: equals, not_equals, less_than, less_than=, greater_than,
  greater_than=, is / is not (aliases), contains, starts with, has key
- Logical: and, or, not
  `less_than=` and `greater_than=` are single tokens; no spaces around `=`.
  Symbolic `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>` are not part of the language.
- Precedence (high to low): `power` (right-assoc), unary `-` / `not`,
  `* / modulo`, `+ -`, comparisons, `not`, `and`, `or`.
- Assignment targets: `name`, `obj.prop`, `key "k" of map`, `map's "k"`,
  `item 1 of list`, `first item of list`, `last item of list`.

3.4 Control flow
- if <condition>:
      ...
  otherwise if <condition>:
      ...
  otherwise:
      ...
- repeat <count> times:
- repeat with <i> from <a> to <b> [by|step <n>] :
- for each <ITEM> in <LIST>:
- while <CONDITION>:
- stop loop / break          leave the innermost loop
- skip next / continue       start the next iteration

3.5 Functions
- define <NAME> with <ARG1>, <ARG2>:
      ...
      give back <VALUE>
  Call them without parentheses: `heal_player with target, 25`. A function with
  no arguments runs when its name appears in an expression (`status_report`).
  A call in expression position requires `with`; comma-separated bare arguments
  (`add 3 5`) are only accepted in statement position.

3.6 Collections and maps
- set items to "sword", "shield"          list literal (comma-separated)
- set m to empty map                      map literal (string keys)
- set key "hp" of m to 100                set a map entry
- set m's "hp" to 100                     equivalent possessive form
- say key "hp" of m / item 2 of items     read a map entry / 1-based list item
- keys of m                               list of string keys
- `add "x" to loot` bootstraps `loot` as a list when it does not exist yet;
  numeric `add` requires the variable to be set first.
- Lists compare structurally; objects/functions compare by identity.

3.7 Modules, errors, files and timers
- use "path.vibe" [as alias]      load a module; exports are its globals
- try:  ...  if error as err: ... catch `fail with "message"` errors
- write <data> to file <path>     file writing (sandbox: `allow_io`)
- read file <path> / file exists <path> / parse json <text> / to json <value>
- wait <seconds> [seconds]        pause; bare `wait` is an engine verb
- after <seconds> seconds:        one-shot timer block
- every <seconds> seconds:        repeating timer block

3.8 Engine events and verbs
- when <event phrase>:            register an event handler. Examples:
      when player touches lava_block:
      when key "space" is pressed:
      when game starts:
      when game updates with delta:
- Engine verbs in standalone mode are logged as `[ENGINE] ...`:
      play boom_sfx / destroy lava_block / jump player with 15
      spawn zombie / move player / rotate lava_block / wait
- Implicit global: `player` is pre-created with `health` 100 so game scripts
  run standalone (its properties can be changed freely).


3.9 Built-in expression forms
- Basic math: round x, floor x, ceil x, abs x, sqrt x, min of a, b,
  max of a, b, clamp v between lo and hi.
- Powers and logs: a power b (right-associative), a modulo b, exp of x,
  log of x (natural log), log base b of x, log10 of x.
- Trigonometry (angles are radians): sin x, cos x, tan x, asin x, acos x,
  atan x, atan2 of y, x. Constants: pi, e.
  Conversions: degrees to radians of x, radians to degrees of x.
- Interpolation: lerp a to b by t — works on numbers and on vec3 values.
- 3D vectors: vec3 x, y, z; magnitude of v (also length of v);
  normalize v; distance between a and b; dot of a and b;
  cross of a and b; angle between a and b (radians).
- Random: random number from a to b, random decimal
- Text: uppercase of t, lowercase of t, trim of t, split t by sep,
  join list with sep, replace a with b in text, length of value
- Time: current time, format time <timestamp>
- Colors: color "red" / color 255, 100, 50
- File/JSON: read file p, file exists p, parse json t, to json v


4. COMPLETE CODE EXAMPLE (game_demo.vibe)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
note: --- 1. VARIABLES & LISTS ---
set player_name to "Ali"
set health to 100
set max_health to 100
set move_speed to 12.5
set is_alive to true

set inventory to "sword", "shield", "potion"
add "gold_key" to inventory

note: --- 2. PRINTING ---
say "Welcome to Azravibe,", player_name
say "Starting health:", health

note: --- 3. CREATING 3D OBJECTS (MAKE) ---
make rounded_box as lava_block
set lava_block.color to "red"
set lava_block.material to "glowing"
set lava_block.position to 0, 5, 10
set lava_block.size to 4, 1, 4

make sound as boom_sfx
set boom_sfx.file to "explosion.wav"

note: --- 4. MATH & CONDITIONS ---
subtract 25 from health

if health less_than= 0:
    set is_alive to false
    error "Player has died!"
otherwise if health less_than 30 and is_alive equals true:
    warn "Low health warning!"
otherwise if health greater_than= max_health:
    say "Full health!"
otherwise:
    say "Current health is", health

note: --- 5. LOOPS ---
repeat 3 times:
    say "Countdown tick..."

for each item in inventory:
    say "Inventory slot:", item

note: --- 6. FUNCTIONS ---
define heal_player with target, amount:
    add amount to target.health
    say "Healed by", amount, "points."
    give back target.health

set updated_hp to heal_player with player, 25

note: --- 7. GAME ENGINE EVENTS ---
when player touches lava_block:
    set player.health to 0
    play boom_sfx
    destroy lava_block
    say "Game Over!"

when key "space" is pressed:
    jump player with 15

note: --- 8. MULTIPLY / DIVIDE & ZERO-ARG FUNCTIONS ---
multiply health by 2
say "Health doubled to:", health
divide health by 2
say "Health back to:", health

define status_report:
    give back "all systems go"

set report to status_report
say "System report:", report


5. IMPLEMENTATION, CLI & RUNTIME MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Milestone 1 (implemented): Core Azra interpreter
- Lexer: tokens for WORD, NUMBER, STRING, INDENT, DEDENT, NEWLINE, punctuation,
  dot, possessive `'s`, and the single-token `less_than=` / `greater_than=`.
- Parser: recursive descent building an AST, with line-numbered diagnostics and
  a maximum nesting depth guard.
- Tree-walking interpreter: evaluates the AST directly with a lexical scope
  chain, checked integer arithmetic, events, timers and mock engine objects.
  There is NO bytecode or stack VM in the current implementation; a stack-based
  bytecode VM is a future milestone, not a shipped feature.
- CLI runner: `azra run script.vibe`, `azra check script.vibe`,
  `azra eval 'say "hi"'`, `azra repl`, or bare `azra` for the REPL.
  `azra run` accepts `--ticks <n>` (engine frames) and a repeatable
  `--trigger "<event phrase>"` flag.
- REPL commands: `:help`, `:vars`, `:reset`, `:clear`, `:quit` / `:exit`,
  and `leave azra` to exit.

Milestone 2 (in progress): Rounded-Voxel 3D Engine
- Graphics: chunked greedy meshing + bevel shader for smooth rounded block edges.
- Physics: RigidBody assemblies (welded blocks), hinges, motors, and collisions.
- Sandboxing: `SandboxConfig` gates I/O and instruction budget; the strict
  profile caps instructions per tick. Default in the standalone CLI is permissive.

Milestone 3 (planned): Azravibe Studio & Azravibe Player
- Studio: Viewport gizmos, scene tree, property inspector, `.vibe` code editor.
- Packaging: Export scenes + `.vibe` scripts into a single `.azv` world bundle.
- Player: Multiplayer client launcher with authoritative server networking.


6. NUMERIC SEMANTICS & EDGE RULES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Integers use i64 and all integer arithmetic is checked: overflow is a runtime
  error, never a silent wrap or a Rust panic.
- `/` of two integers truncates toward zero (`say 7 / 2` -> `3`). Give either
  operand a decimal point for fractional division (`say 7 / 2.0` -> `3.5`).
- `modulo` uses the Euclidean rule for both integers and floats: the result is
  non-negative for a positive divisor (`say -5 modulo 2` -> `1`).
- `power` with a zero base and a negative exponent is a division-by-zero error.
- NaN can arise from invalid float operations such as `-8 power 0.5`. NaN cannot
  be ordered: comparisons, `min`/`max`, `clamp` bounds and `sort` report a
  runtime error instead of silently producing an arbitrary answer.
- Loop bodies have a 10,000,000-iteration safety cap per loop; the REPL and the
  strict sandbox impose a smaller instruction budget. The permissive CLI sandbox
  has no global instruction budget, so use strict mode for untrusted scripts.
- Scope: `if` and `try` blocks share the enclosing scope, while `for each` and
  `repeat with` create a child scope so their loop variable does not leak.
  `set` inside a function updates the nearest existing binding or creates a new
  local; it never creates a global.
- `+` concatenates when either operand is a string (`"hp: " + 75` -> `"hp: 75"`);
  all other arithmetic operators require numbers.
- Comparisons between integers use exact i64 values; mixed integer/float
  comparisons convert through f64 and can lose precision above 2^53.
- Domain errors are reported as catchable runtime errors: `sqrt` of a negative
  number, `asin`/`acos` outside [-1, 1], `log` of a non-positive number,
  a `log` base that is non-positive or 1, `exp` overflow, normalizing or
  measuring the angle of a zero-length vector.
- `atan2 of y, x` uses the two-argument arctangent, so it handles all quadrants.
  `angle between` returns radians and always lies in [0, pi].
- Deep recursion: the CLI runs scripts on a 64 MiB stack with a call-depth cap
  of 512 (enough for Ackermann(3,3)); embedders using the library default get
  a cap of 64. Exceeding the cap is a fatal, uncatchable runtime error.
- Runnable stress suites live in `tests/vibe-test/*.vibe` and are executed by
  `cargo test --test vibe_scripts`: advanced math, recursion, a 1..10000 prime
  sieve, Newton-Raphson, quadratics, 3D projectile physics and error handling.
  Two longer demonstrations are also included:
    `09_euler500.vibe`  — smallest N with exactly 2^500500 divisors, computed
                          entirely in Azra as the product of the 500500
                          smallest p^(2^k) values; result modulo 500500507 is
                          `35407281`. Run it with `azra run` (about 9 s in a
                          release build).
    `10_three_body.vibe` — Newtonian simulation of masses 3, 4, 5 at rest on a
                          3-4-5 right triangle, adaptive RK4, stops when a
                          close binary forms or a star is ejected. It reports
                          a maximum relative energy drift near 8e-10.


7. TESTING, PACKAGES AND STANDARD LIBRARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Test declarations: `test "name":` followed by an indented body. Test blocks
  are registered while a file loads and executed by `azra test <file-or-dir>`.
- Assertions: `assert CONDITION`, `assert CONDITION, "message"`,
  `assert equals A, B` and `assert not_equals A, B` (all accept an optional
  final message expression). A failed assertion is a catchable runtime error.
  `std/testing` adds `check`, `check_equal` and `check_close` helpers.
- Project manifest `azra.toml` has `[package]` (name, version, description,
  entry) and `[dependencies]`. A dependency is either `"1.2"` (local registry,
  ^1.2 semantics) or `{ path = "../local", version = "0.1.0" }`.
- `azra init [name]`, `azra pkg publish <dir>`, `azra pkg add`,
  `azra pkg remove`, `azra pkg install`, `azra pkg tree` and `azra pkg list`
  manage packages. Installation writes `azra.lock` and copies packages into
  `.azra/packages/`.
- `use "std/math"`, `std/collections`, `std/string`, `std/vec3`,
  `std/random`, `std/io`, `std/json`, `std/testing` load bundled standard
  library modules. Installed packages load with `use "package-name/module"`.


8. DEVELOPER TOOLING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- `azra fmt [--check] <file.vibe>` normalizes indentation to 4 spaces, expands
  tabs and strips trailing whitespace.
- `azra-lsp` is a dependency-free language server (JSON-RPC over stdio) with
  diagnostics, completion, hover, go-to-definition and document formatting.
  Editor clients: `editors/vscode/` and `editors/neovim/`.
- `playground/` runs a Monaco editor plus the interpreter compiled to
  WebAssembly (`src/wasm.rs`), with shareable URLs; all execution is local.
- Project website and hosted playground: https://azravibe.com/vibe
- Source and contributions: https://github.com/11nawid/azravibe.vibe
